142
00:06:44,280 --> 00:06:49,370
OK, so that's phase one, immediate accountability, clear language, setting the stage for recovery.

143
00:06:49,790 --> 00:06:54,270
Now phase two, strategic communication and stakeholder management.

144
00:06:54,430 --> 00:06:54,650
Right.

145
00:06:54,710 --> 00:06:55,930
You've owned it internally.

146
00:06:55,950 --> 00:07:01,990
Now you need to manage how it looks externally, especially to the client, and keep things stable inside the company.

147
00:07:02,210 --> 00:07:03,470
Let's talk about the client first.

148
00:07:03,630 --> 00:07:06,390
The sources really stress honesty and crucially timing.

149
00:07:07,020 --> 00:07:10,600
Honesty within the hour keeps confidence intact despite today's stumble.

150
00:07:10,620 --> 00:07:11,360
Within the hour.

151
00:07:11,940 --> 00:07:13,420
That's not just a random time frame.

152
00:07:13,730 --> 00:07:14,610
It's about control.

153
00:07:14,990 --> 00:07:18,150
If you wait too long, the client might discover the error themselves.

154
00:07:18,770 --> 00:07:24,000
Maybe their finance department flags a weird invoice or they notice the shipment hasn't arrived.

155
00:07:24,050 --> 00:07:26,690
Ah, and finding out secondhand is always worse.

156
00:07:26,910 --> 00:07:27,450
Much worse.

157
00:07:27,970 --> 00:07:31,660
When you deliver the bad news proactively, you control the narrative.

158
00:07:32,260 --> 00:07:34,370
You show you're on top of it, vigilant.

159
00:07:34,890 --> 00:07:37,030
And the action required is specific, too.

160
00:07:37,070 --> 00:07:42,780
I'll call their project lead, apologize plainly, and share the corrected statement while we talk.

161
00:07:43,140 --> 00:07:45,130
Why, apologize plainly.

162
00:07:45,290 --> 00:07:46,860
Plain language cuts through.

163
00:07:46,890 --> 00:07:54,330
