164
00:07:54,390 --> 00:07:54,630
Right.

165
00:07:54,750 --> 00:07:55,810
It sounds insincere.

166
00:07:56,050 --> 00:07:56,530
Exactly.

167
00:07:56,710 --> 00:07:58,620
A simple direct apology says, we get it.

168
00:07:58,780 --> 00:08:00,480
We understand this affects you.

169
00:08:00,460 --> 00:08:04,530
And crucially, they immediately link that apology to proving it won't happen again.

170
00:08:04,550 --> 00:08:05,230
Oh.

171
00:08:05,250 --> 00:08:09,700
By promising to start auditing similar invoices so nothing else blindsides their finance crew.

172
00:08:09,820 --> 00:08:10,520
Oh, that's clever.

173
00:08:10,560 --> 00:08:13,380
It shows the mistake is already driving broader improvements.

174
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:15,030
You're not just fixing this one thing.

175
00:08:15,310 --> 00:08:17,470
You're strengthening the whole system because of it.

176
00:08:17,870 --> 00:08:21,620
It turns a negative into a demonstration of diligence.

177
00:08:21,640 --> 00:08:24,780
And then there's the follow-up, offering a 15-minute sink.

178
00:08:24,810 --> 00:08:27,420
Yeah, just 15 minutes seems short.

179
00:08:27,560 --> 00:08:28,160
That's the point.

180
00:08:28,300 --> 00:08:28,980
It's tactical.

181
00:08:29,580 --> 00:08:33,300
A 15-minute sync signals control and respect for their time.

182
00:08:33,420 --> 00:08:35,040
It says, we've got this handled.

183
00:08:35,120 --> 00:08:35,780
Here's the update.

184
00:08:35,850 --> 00:08:36,550
We know you're busy.

185
00:08:37,110 --> 00:08:42,750
Ah, whereas an hour-long meeting might suggest panic, like, oh, God, we need to have a massive damage control session.

186
00:08:42,810 --> 00:08:43,350
Precisely.

187
00:08:43,850 --> 00:08:47,800
The short sync shows we're committed to stabilizing operations efficiently.

188
00:08:48,600 --> 00:08:52,100
It offers availability without suggesting the problem is spiraling.

189
00:08:52,400 --> 00:09:00,360
And connected to that, they're thinking about the financial side, too, asking, do we need to offer a service credit or will swift corrections satisfy their leadership?

190
00:09:00,540 --> 00:09:00,980
Right.

191
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:02,400
Making the response proportional.

192
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:04,880
Is a quick, clean fix enough?

193
00:09:05,590 --> 00:09:09,410
Or was the impact significant enough to warrant a concession like a service credit?

194
00:09:09,490 --> 00:09:11,700
But you can't just offer things randomly, right?

195
00:09:11,830 --> 00:09:12,970
No, definitely not.

196
00:09:13,350 --> 00:09:14,190
The guidance is clear.

197
00:09:14,700 --> 00:09:18,650
Any concession has to align with our contracts and precedent.

198
00:09:18,670 --> 00:09:20,410
You need to check with legal or compliance.

199
00:09:21,010 --> 00:09:27,170
Sincerity is crucial, but the response also has to be strategically sound and contractually viable.

200
00:09:27,420 --> 00:09:29,640
Okay, that covers the external communication.

201
00:09:29,710 --> 00:09:34,200
What about internally, managing stakeholders inside the company?

202
00:09:34,460 --> 00:09:35,220
Equally critical.

203
00:09:35,720 --> 00:09:38,450
The rule here is transparency to avoid surprises later.

204
00:09:39,090 --> 00:09:44,260
As the source says, loop them in now, surprises later would erode trust we worked hard to rebuild.

205
00:09:44,350 --> 00:09:44,850
Hmm.

206
00:09:44,870 --> 00:09:46,770
Trust is hard won, easily lost.

207
00:09:47,050 --> 00:09:47,430
Exactly.

208
00:09:47,710 --> 00:09:49,510
Think about, say, the product leads.

209
00:09:49,970 --> 00:09:53,090
If they're planning a launch based on that faulty dashboard data...

210
00:09:53,110 --> 00:09:54,520
They're building on shaky ground.

211
00:09:54,760 --> 00:09:55,020
Right.

212
00:09:55,080 --> 00:10:00,320
So sharing the issue in the remediation timeline keeps their planning grounded in reality.

213
00:10:00,640 --> 00:10:04,970
You're treating the mistake not as a secret shame, but as shared operational knowledge.

214
00:10:04,990 --> 00:10:07,630
But isn't there a risk in telling everyone internally straight away?

215
00:10:07,750 --> 00:10:13,360
Couldn't it cause unnecessary panic or draw too much scrutiny before you even fully understand the root cause?

216
00:10:13,570 --> 00:10:15,490
That's where tiered communication comes in.

217
00:10:15,910 --> 00:10:25,500
You immediately loop in the people directly affected or involved in the fix, like finance, operations, relevant product leads, maybe not a company-wide broadcast initially.

218
00:10:25,680 --> 00:10:25,800
Okay.

219
00:10:26,020 --> 00:10:27,150
Targeted transparency.

220
00:10:27,210 --> 00:10:27,390
Yes.

221
00:10:27,850 --> 00:10:30,170
And you provide a clear, consistent message.

222
00:10:31,310 --> 00:10:36,150
For tricky issues like myth billing with potential legal implications, the protocol is specific.

223
00:10:36,980 --> 00:10:41,740
Loop legal by sharing the draft email, but keep the client call personal and sincere.

224
00:10:41,840 --> 00:10:45,830
Ah, so legal checks, the wording, the risk, the policy.

225
00:10:46,170 --> 00:10:51,090
But the relationship owner delivers the message personally, maintaining that human connection.

226
00:10:51,320 --> 00:10:52,920
That distinction feels important.

227
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:54,550
Legal covers the formal side.

228
00:10:54,590 --> 00:10:56,670
The leader handles the relationship tone.

229
00:10:56,890 --> 00:10:57,430
Absolutely.

230
00:10:57,770 --> 00:11:00,340
And making sure everyone's singing from the same hymn sheet is vital.

231
00:11:00,820 --> 00:11:04,080
That's why posting key takeaways in a shared leadership channel is mentioned.

232
00:11:04,700 --> 00:11:05,500
One source of truth.

233
00:11:05,660 --> 00:11:06,000
Got it.

234
00:11:06,100 --> 00:11:09,130
So clear communication internally and externally.

235
00:11:09,550 --> 00:11:10,970
What's the final piece of this phase?

236
00:11:11,250 --> 00:11:11,950
Documentation.

237
00:11:12,370 --> 00:11:12,650
Proof.

238
00:11:13,070 --> 00:11:14,700
You have to document the recovery process.

239
00:11:14,720 --> 00:11:16,060
Not just for now, but for later.

240
00:11:16,360 --> 00:11:16,840
Both.

241
00:11:17,460 --> 00:11:27,670
For immediate stakeholders, so everyone knows what's happening, and definitely for future audits or reviews, summarizing notes in the incident channel creates that real-time log.

242
00:11:28,070 --> 00:11:33,340
And the sources mention specific metrics for updates, like for the next day's stand-up meeting.

243
00:11:33,540 --> 00:11:39,010
Yeah, something like, I'll gather variance totals and note expected recovery by Friday for transparency.

244
00:11:39,830 --> 00:11:43,460
Setting that recovery by Friday target gives everyone a concrete goal.

245
00:11:44,020 --> 00:11:47,440
Focus shifts from the past mistake to the future resolution.

246
00:11:47,760 --> 00:11:48,640
And for the auditors?

247
00:11:48,880 --> 00:11:50,190
Concrete evidence is king.

248
00:11:50,570 --> 00:11:53,630
They commit to capturing screenshots, documenting changes.

249
00:11:54,290 --> 00:11:59,600
Because, as the source notes, auditors appreciate concrete evidence when revisiting quarterly controls.

250
00:11:59,660 --> 00:12:01,550
It proves the six wasn't just talk.

251
00:12:01,570 --> 00:12:04,350
It was implemented, logged, verifiable.

252
00:12:04,530 --> 00:12:05,010
Exactly.

253
00:12:05,170 --> 00:12:08,520
It demonstrates a high level of professional discipline and accountability.

